          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Reviewers often describe a peak performance window of roughly 100 to 200 hours of intensive play for typical paddles, after which feel and spin begin to change. Some foam-core paddles are reported to last considerably longer. These figures are rough and vary widely by construction, play style and how the paddle is stored."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is core crush on a pickleball paddle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Core crush is a term players use for the honeycomb core losing its springiness after repeated impacts. Crushed areas play differently from the rest of the face, often producing a dead spot with weaker, duller shots. In some paddles core breakdown can instead make the face feel springier and more powerful."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know if my pickleball paddle has a dead spot?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Watch for shots from one part of the face that come off with less pace and a duller sound than the rest, often near the center. Gently tapping across the face and listening for a duller tone can help locate it. Comparing with a new paddle of the same model is the most reliable check."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does pickleball paddle grit wear off?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Textured faces lose some of their grip with use, starting where you hit most often, and many players notice reduced spin within the first few dozen hours of hard play on heavily textured faces. Cleaning can restore grip lost to dirt, but worn texture cannot practically be restored."
          }
